11
00:01:38.796 --> 00:01:53.623
<v Maya>And that is the core of our mission today. We're looking at a landmark August 2026 paper published in Nature Human Behavior by Serati and a massive team of researchers. We are also bringing in five foundational papers to give us the history. And the</v>

12
00:01:53.663 --> 00:02:02.207
<v Maya>single profound question we're trying to answer is this. When you ask an AI model to polish your writing, it keeps what you meant. But does it keep who you are?</v>

13
00:02:02.795 --> 00:02:17.723
<v Daniel>And as this new paper proves, the answer is no, not entirely. And for the first time, we actually have the tools to measure exactly how much of you goes missing. But to really grasp why this matters, we have to rewind a bit. We need to step back to a foundational paper</v>

14
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:32.411
<v Daniel>by Kaczynski from 2013. Because without this context, the 2026 paper doesn't really have the same stakes. Kaczynski proved that our ordinary everyday digital traces, like our subtle language choices, the words we favor, even our</v>

