130
00:19:50.912 --> 00:20:05.155
<v Daniel>It's a genuinely compelling argument. And the Cerati paper even concedes that these cues are double edged and weakening them reduces surveillance and discrimination risks. But I would push back hard against calling it a true privacy win. And it all comes back to that</v>

131
00:20:05.615 --> 00:20:09.236
<v Daniel>directional bias finding. It would be a privacy feature if the AI made you invisible.</v>

132
00:20:09.396 --> 00:20:09.576
<v Maya>Right.</v>

133
00:20:09.896 --> 00:20:24.641
<v Daniel>If it just blurred you into static or a perfectly neutral average. But it doesn't do that. It averages you into an older, liberal, unemotional, moralizing man. You aren't achieving true privacy. You're just wearing a cultural costume that you didn't consent to put on, you are trading</v>

134
00:20:24.701 --> 00:20:25.821
<v Daniel>surveillance for erasure.</v>

135
00:20:26.021 --> 00:20:40.867
<v Maya>A cultural costume? Wow. Trading surveillance for erasure. That is an incredibly heavy trade-off. I mean, we're fixing our grammar. We're getting the promotion, hiding from the algorithm, but at the cost of painting over our own fingerprints with someone else's. It really makes you think about the</v>

136
00:20:40.947 --> 00:20:53.375
<v Maya>friction we used to have in writing and what that friction actually produced. It produced us. Next time you click improve readability on an email, ask yourself, Are you just fixing your grammar or are you quietly letting the machine decide who you get to be today?</v>
